SPEC = {'setting': 'Use the approved reference for thin warm-brown linework and matte Japanese animation style only. Single close-up, warm ivory, necessary food/tools only. No arrows, diagrams, text or insets. Each cooking stage is separate: rice cooked plain, all octopus tender-cooked outside rice, dried, browned, glazed then folded in.', 'steps': ['A wooden cutting board with about ten raw baby octopus (iidako), cleaned: each one SMALL — about 6 cm long including its tentacles, its rounded head about the size of a walnut — translucent grey-mauve with paler cream suckers, a smooth glossy rounded head and EIGHT short tentacles; simple clean shapes, never slimy-looking, never creepy, no ink, cleaned and lying in two loose rows, heads the right way out; a pair of kitchen scissors resting at the edge of the board. Nothing else.', 'A white bowl holding about ten raw baby octopus (iidako), cleaned: each one SMALL — about 6 cm long including its tentacles, its rounded head about the size of a walnut — translucent grey-mauve with paler cream suckers, a smooth glossy rounded head and EIGHT short tentacles; simple clean shapes, never slimy-looking, never creepy, no ink, just rinsed: clean, wet and glistening, a few water droplets, no foam and no salt left. Nothing else.', "A wooden cutting board with one neat heap (about 300 g) of raw baby octopus cut into 1 cm pieces — each a little grey-mauve chunk with a sucker or two (raw octopus cut into 1 cm pieces: small irregular grey-mauve CHUNKS (short cut lengths of tentacle and bits of head), each showing a pale cut face and maybe one sucker — NEVER tiny whole octopuses, NEVER little octopus shapes with heads and legs); a chef's knife resting along the top edge. Nothing else — NO whole octopus.", 'Tight close-up on a small pale board: a right-handed cook cross-chopping thin ginger matchsticks into FINE1–2mm mince with ONE chef knife. Safe tucked left claw hand behind blade. A small neat pile of minced pale-yellow ginger beside, each readable cut piece, no long strips in finished pile. Plain ivory, no octopus or rice.', 'Tight overhead view of a rice cooker inner pot with freshly cooked PLAIN WHITE short-grain rice, gentle soft mounds and a few dozen readable grains on its surface; a right hand fluffs with one white rice paddle, left hand steadies pot. Absolutely NO octopus, broth, brown liquid, ginger strips, sauce, soy or purple tint anywhere. Smooth clean hand-drawn surfaces.', 'The small stainless saucepan on the gas hob with a LOW blue flame: a pile of 1 cm pink cooked octopus pieces in clear water, a few small separate bubbles at the edge, a wisp of steam. Nothing else — no sauce yet, NO whole octopus.', 'Close-up of right hand turning small1cm tender-cooked OCTOPUS PIECES with metal tongs in a black skillet, left hand holds pan handle. Pieces are short angular bits of tentacle and mantle with at most one visible sucker each, lightly browned edges and pale interiors. Sparse single layer, a thin neutral oil film, no simmering liquid, no glaze yet, NO whole octopus animals or heads, no rice. Natural five-digit hands, no duplicate tools.', 'Tight close-up of small1cm already browned octopus pieces tossed in a black skillet with a VERY THIN reddish-brown sweet-soy ginger glaze clinging to their surfaces. A right hand uses a broad wooden spatula, left hand holds handle. A few small1–2mm ginger pieces, no long strips. Pan bottom clearly visible, no liquid bath or watery pool, no whole octopus, no rice or green onion yet. Restrained matte culinary illustration, no black burnt coating.', 'Tight instructional close-up of a wide white bowl holding FLUFFY COOKED WHITE short-grain rice and small1cm caramel-brown glazed OCTOPUS PIECES scattered through it, plus a few sliced green-onion rings. Right hand folds with one white rice paddle edge-first, left hand steadies bowl. Rice stays mostly WHITE with only occasional light tan contact marks, never uniformly purple or brown. No liquid pouring, no sauce puddle, no ginger matchsticks, no whole octopus. Few dozen readable grains, not dense microtexture.', 'Tight close-up of two natural adult hands gently shaping ONE soft TRIANGLE of mostly white short-grain rice with a few small caramel-brown octopus pieces and green-onion rings mixed through. Left palm cups underneath; right fingers form a loose roof over top corner. Exactly one thumb and four proportionate fingers per hand with natural occlusion. No nori yet, no whole octopus on top, no sauce pouring, no purple rice, no diagram or arrows.'], 'mise': ['a white plate with a heap of raw octopus cut into 1 cm pieces: small irregular grey-mauve CHUNKS (short cut lengths of tentacle and bits of head), each showing a pale cut face and maybe one sucker — NEVER tiny whole octopuses, NEVER little octopus shapes with heads and legs', 'a fine-mesh strainer of washed raw short-grain rice, opaque white, a soft mound with a few dozen outlined grains', 'Square ingredient preview, one small white plate holding a modest loose pile of FINELY MINCED pale-yellow fresh ginger,1–2mm short irregular cut pieces, no long matchsticks, no whole ginger root, no hands or tools. Clean warm ivory background, countable sparse pieces.', 'one small white cup of reduced sweet-soy ginger glaze, smooth thin dark brown sauce', 'one small white plate of sliced green-onion rings', 'one white plate with6 narrow matte dark-green nori strips'], 'how': {1: [('Snip across just behind the eyes, where the head joins the body.', 'Close-up over the wooden board: the LEFT hand holds one raw baby octopus (iidako), cleaned: each one SMALL — about 6 cm long including its tentacles, its rounded head about the size of a walnut — translucent grey-mauve with paler cream suckers, a smooth glossy rounded head and EIGHT short tentacles; simple clean shapes, never slimy-looking, never creepy, no ink by its tentacles, head up; the RIGHT hand snips with small kitchen scissors straight across the narrow neck just behind the two eyes, where the round head joins the body. Nothing else.'), ('Turn the head inside out with a chopstick and pull away the innards.', 'Close-up: the LEFT hand holds a raw grey-mauve baby octopus (about 6 cm) by its tentacles; the RIGHT hand pushes ONE wooden chopstick into the top of its head, turning the head inside out like a sock, the pale inner lining showing. A small amount of innards, drawn simply and neatly (no blood, no gore). Nothing else.'), ('Squeeze the hard beak out from the centre of the tentacles.', 'Close-up: a raw grey-mauve baby octopus (about 6 cm) held upside down in the LEFT hand, tentacles spread open like a little flower; the RIGHT thumb and index finger pinch the centre where the tentacles meet, and a tiny dark beak (the size of a peppercorn) pops out. Nothing else.')], 2: [('Rub hard with coarse salt for 1–2 minutes, until a grey foam comes up.', "Close-up: in a white bowl, the RIGHT hand rubs and kneads about ten raw baby octopus (iidako), cleaned: each one SMALL — about 6 cm long including its tentacles, its rounded head about the size of a walnut — translucent grey-mauve with paler cream suckers, a smooth glossy rounded head and EIGHT short tentacles; simple clean shapes, never slimy-looking, never creepy, no ink with coarse salt (a few dozen separate white salt crystals); a little light-grey foam clings to them (a thin soft film, NOT froth, NOT bubbles). The LEFT hand steadies the bowl's rim. Nothing else."), ('Run your fingers down each tentacle to clean the suckers, then rinse well.', 'Close-up over a white bowl under a thin stream of running tap water: the LEFT hand holds one raw baby octopus (iidako), cleaned: each one SMALL — about 6 cm long including its tentacles, its rounded head about the size of a walnut — translucent grey-mauve with paler cream suckers, a smooth glossy rounded head and EIGHT short tentacles; simple clean shapes, never slimy-looking, never creepy, no ink by the head; the RIGHT thumb and index finger slide down one tentacle from base to tip. Nothing else.')], 3: [('Cut the rest into 1 cm pieces.', "Close-up on the wooden board: the RIGHT hand cuts one raw baby octopus (iidako), cleaned: each one SMALL — about 6 cm long including its tentacles, its rounded head about the size of a walnut — translucent grey-mauve with paler cream suckers, a smooth glossy rounded head and EIGHT short tentacles; simple clean shapes, never slimy-looking, never creepy, no ink into 1 cm pieces with a chef's knife; the LEFT hand holds it in a CLAW GRIP — fingertips curled under and tucked, knuckles against the flat of the blade. A few cut pieces beside it. Nothing else.")]}, 'hero': 'Landscape3:2 close-up of ONE finished baby-octopus onigiri on a simple ivory plate. One neat cut half in front reveals mostly WHITE cooked short-grain rice with small1cm caramel-brown glazed OCTOPUS PIECES evenly mixed INSIDE and a few green-onion rings, not a central pocket. The other half stands behind, soft rounded triangle with a narrow matte dark-green nori band round lower third. No whole octopus, heads, tentacles hanging out, purple rice, pink-purple broth, labels or hands. Warm clean hand-drawn Japanese animation, a few dozen crisp outlined rice grains, no recursive tiny texture.'}
